-- §9 bytestrings: real string work goes through opaque Atom handles
-- over a runtime side-table. bytesBind registers the UTF-8 encoding of
-- a codepoint list under a (compile-time-chosen) atom and emits a
-- (Bytes, H) completion tuple into Global — the gate consumers join
-- on, since binds are deferred effects (§7.2). say %b decodes a
-- handle; == stays pure atom identity, so content comparison is the
-- bytesEqual verb's job (§9). bytesRead(H) is the decode-back path
-- (§9, issue #12): the same codepoints come out that went in, so a
-- string in and out of ByteString is the identity.

: [bytesBind Greeting [72, 105]; (Go,)]

(Bytes, Greeting), (Go,) : [say "greeting: %b" Greeting; (Check,)]

-- identical bytes under a different handle: bytesEqual compares
-- contents, == compares handles — the two verdicts disagree (§9)
: [bytesBind Salute [72, 105]; (Go2,)]
(Bytes, Salute), (Check,), (Go2,) :
  [ if bytesEqual(Greeting, Salute) then say "contents equal" else say "contents differ"
  ; if Greeting == Salute then say "same atom?!" else say "distinct atoms"
  ; (Read,)
  ; exit 0 ]

-- the identity invariant (§9, issue #12): bytesRead decodes the
-- handle's bytes back into the codepoint list the bind consumed —
-- here it matches the string literal pattern "Hi" structurally, and
-- %s decodes it like any casual string (the Greeting bind has already
-- landed — the "%b" say above decoded it — so (Read,) is a sound gate)
(Read,) : (RoundTrip, bytesRead(Greeting))
(RoundTrip, "Hi") : [say "round trip: %s" bytesRead(Greeting); (Fin,)]
(Fin,) : exit 0
